[46.972,"o"," succeeded in 0ms:\r\n./backlog/tasks/parallix-adhoc-0001 - fix-hello-world-greeting.md\r\n./config/agents.json\r\n./.git\r\n./.gitignore\r\n./hello.sh\r\n./missions/parallix-adhoc-0001/MISSION.md\r\n./scripts/verify-local.sh\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/goals_1.sqlite\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/goals_1.sqlite-shm\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/goals_1.sqlite-wal\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/installation_id\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/logs_2.sqlite\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/logs_2.sqlite-shm\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/logs_2.sqlite-wal\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/memories_1.sqlite\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/memories_1.sqlite-shm\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/memories_1.sqlite-wal\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/models_cache.json\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/queue_1.sqlite\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/queue_1.sqlite-shm\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/queue_1.sqlite-wal\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/.sandbox_migration\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/state_5.sqlite\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/state_5.sqlite-shm\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/state_5.sqlite-wal\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/thread_history_1.sqlite\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/thread_history_1.sqlite-shm\r\n./.workflow/codex-home/.codex/thread_history_1.sqlite-wal\r\n./workflow.config.json\r\n--- missions/parallix-adhoc-0001/MISSION.md\r\n# Mission: Fix the misspelled hello-world greeting in hello.sh (parallix-adhoc-0001)\r\n\r\n## Goal\r\n"]
[49.972,"o","`hello.sh` prints the greeting `Helo, Wrld!` — both words are misspelled. Correct the greeting so that running `bash hello.sh` emits exactly `Hello, World!` followed by a single newline, with no other change to the script's behaviour, structure, or exit status (0).\r\n\r\n## Why Now\r\n`hello.sh` is the only executable artifact in this repository and is the canonical smoke target for the Parallix workflow demo. A visibly misspelled greeting makes the repo unusable as a reference example, and any downstream demo, screenshot, or smoke assertion that quotes the expected output is wrong until the string is corrected. The defect is a two-character typo with no dependencies, so it can be closed in a single checkpoint rather than carried as open debt.\r\n\r\n## Refinement Signals\r\n- Predicted NEL bucket: Small (0–80)\r\n- Confidence: High\r\n- Selection note: activate as-is\r\n- Main drivers: single-file change to one `printf` string literal in `hello.sh`; no build system, no dependency graph, no test harness to extend; verification is a direct output comparison of `bash hello.sh`\r\n\r\n## Scope\r\n- Correct the greeting string in `hello.sh` so the script's stdout is exactly `Hello, World!\\n`.\r\n- Keep the existing `printf` invocation and the trailing `\\n` escape rather than switching to `echo` or another emitter.\r\n- Keep the existing `# Hello world` comment line and the blank line that follows it.\r\n- Update the backlog task `backlog/tasks/parallix-adhoc-0001 - fix-hello-world-greeting.md` Description with the corrected before/after strings.\r\n\r\n## Out of Scope\r\n- Adding a shebang, `set -euo pipefail`, or any other hardening to `hello.sh` — the file has no shebang today and gaining one is a separate decision.\r\n- Introducing a test framework, `test/` directory, `package.json`, or CI configuration; this repository has none and the mission does not create one.\r\n- Changing `scripts/verify-local.sh`, `workflow.config.json`, `config/agents.json`, or `.gitignore`.\r\n- Internationalising, parameterising, or templating the greeting (for example accepting a name argument).\r\n- Generating a `graphify-out/graph.json`; the directory is empty and stays empty.\r\n\r\n## Success Criteria\r\n> **Falsifiability rule (ADR 0039 Part 2):** Each criterion must be falsifiable. Forbidden: subjective adjectives (\"easy, fast, simple, intuitive, user-friendly, responsive, quick, efficient\" without an attached metric) and vague quantifiers (\"multiple, several, some, many, few, various\"). For refactor / condense / migration missions, the criterion must enumerate the specific elements (rules, files, behaviours) that must survive — generic phrases like \"preserve critical content\" are not sufficient.\r\n\r\n1. `bash hello.sh` writes exactly the 14 bytes `Hello, World!\\n` to stdout — verifiable with `bash hello.sh | od -c` or `[ \"$(bash hello.sh)\" = \"Hello, World!\" ]`.\r\n2. `bash hello.sh` writes nothing to stderr and exits with status 0 — verifiable with `bash hello.sh 2>&1 >/dev/null | wc -c` returning `0` and `echo $?` returning `0`.\r\n3. The strings `Helo` and `Wrld` no longer appear anywhere in the repository working tree — verifiable with `git grep -n -e Helo -e Wrld` returning no matches (exit status 1).\r\n4. `hello.sh` still emits its greeting via `printf` with an explicit `\\n` escape and no shebang line — verifiable with `git grep -n 'printf \"Hello, World!\\\\n\"' hello.sh` matching and `head -1 hello.sh` not starting with `#!`.\r\n5. `git diff --stat main...HEAD -- hello.sh` reports exactly 1 file changed, 1 insertion, 1 deletion — the fix touches one line of one source file.\r\n6. Files other than `hello.sh`, `missions/parallix-adhoc-0001/**`, and `backlog/tasks/parallix-adhoc-0001 - fix-hello-world-greeting.md` are unmodified — verifiable with `git diff --name-only main...HEAD`.\r\n7. `./scripts/verify-local.sh all` exits 0 on the final tree.\r\n\r\n## Risks and Assumptions\r\n- Assumption: the intended greeting is the conventional `Hello, World!` with a comma, a space, and a trailing exclamation mark. The backlog task says only \"fix hello world greeting\"; the misspellings `Helo`"]
[52.972,"o","/`Wrld` make the intent unambiguous, and the file's own comment reads `# Hello world`.\r\n- Assumption: capitalisation is `Hello, World!` (capital H, capital W), matching the file comment's subject and the universal convention, rather than lowercase `world`.\r\n- Risk: `scripts/verify-local.sh` is a stub that unconditionally `exit 0`, so the gate cannot catch a wrong greeting. Mitigation: criteria 1–4 are asserted directly against `bash hello.sh` output and `git grep` in the checkpoint evidence, not delegated to the gate.\r\n- Risk: an over-eager implementer rewrites `hello.sh` (shebang, `set -e`, `echo`, quoting changes) and inflates the diff. Mitigation: criteria 4 and 5 pin the emitter form and the one-line diff size.\r\n- Assumption: `hello.sh` has no execute bit today and does not need one; it is invoked as `bash hello.sh`. Changing file mode would show in `git diff` and violate criterion 5's intent.\r\n- Risk: the file could be committed with CRLF line endings, making the output 15 bytes. Mitigation: criterion 1 checks byte count via `od -c`.\r\n\r\n## Checkpoints\r\n- CP 1: Correct the greeting string in `hello.sh` from `Helo, Wrld!` to `Hello, World!`, leaving the `# Hello world` comment, the blank line, and the `printf` form intact. Capture `bash hello.sh | od -c`, `git grep -n -e Helo -e Wrld` (expected: no match), and `git diff --stat` as evidence for criteria 1–5.\r\n- CP 2: Update the Description in `backlog/tasks/parallix-adhoc-0001 - fix-hello-world-greeting.md` to state the corrected string, run `./scripts/verify-local.sh all`, and confirm `git diff --name-only main...HEAD` lists only `hello.sh`, the mission directory, and the backlog task file (criteria 6–7).\r\n\r\n### Checkpoint Documentation Requirements\r\nEvery checkpoint document (CP-N.md) MUST include:\r\n- A summary of work done — for CP 1, name the exact before and after greeting strings; for CP 2, name every file listed by `git diff --name-only main...HEAD`.\r\n- A `## Goal Check` section, using that exact heading.\r\n- A 3-column pipe-delimited markdown table with columns: Criterion | Evidence | Status — that is, a header row of exactly `| Criterion | Evidence | Status |`.\r\n- At least one evidence row per criterion using durable, verifiable references. Parallix already accepts:\r\n  1. **Recognized repo commands or paths** — for this mission the durable forms are `` `bash hello.sh` ``, `` `bash hello.sh | od -c` ``, `` `git grep -n -e Helo -e Wrld` ``, `` `git diff --stat main...HEAD -- hello.sh` ``, `` `git diff --name-only main...HEAD` ``, `` `./scripts/verify-local.sh all` ``, and the path `hello.sh`\r\n  2. **Test names** — none exist in this repository; do not invent one\r\n  3. **Test file paths** — this repository has no `test/` directory, so cite `hello.sh` and the commands above instead\r\n  4. **ADR references** — e.g., `ADR 0039`; cite an ADR only if the corresponding file exists under `docs/adr/`, which it does not in this repository today\r\n  5. **File:line references** — e.g., `hello.sh:3`; accepted when needed, but line numbers eventually rot, so prefer the command and path forms above\r\n- Raw `stat`/`ls` output or generic prose may appear as supplemental context, but pair them with one of the accepted references above. Pasting `ls -l hello.sh`, `stat hello.sh`, or a sentence such as \"the greeting is now correct\" with no backticked command or path is NOT sufficient evidence and will fail the goal check — every row must carry a runnable command or a repository path.\r\n- A non-generic `Next action:` line at the bottom — name the next concrete step (for CP 1, updating the backlog task Description and running the gate; for CP 2, handing the mission to review), not \"continue\" or \"proceed\".\r\n\r\nExample Goal Check table:\r\n\r\n| Criterion | Evidence | Status |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `bash hello.sh` emits exactly `Hello, World!\\n` | `bash hello.sh \\| od -c` shows `H e l l o ,   W o r l d ! \\n` (14 bytes) | PASS |\r\n| `Helo`/`Wrld` gone from the tree | `git grep -n -e Helo -e Wrld` exits 1 with no output | PASS |\r\n| Fix is a one-line change to one file | `git dif"]
[55.972,"o","f --stat main...HEAD -- hello.sh` reports 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-) | PASS |\r\n| Verification gate ran | `./scripts/verify-local.sh all` | PASS |\r\n\r\n## Gates\r\n- [ ] ./scripts/verify-local.sh all\r\n\r\n## Restricted Areas\r\n- `scripts/verify-local.sh` — the verification gate itself; editing it would let the mission mark its own homework.\r\n- `workflow.config.json` and `config/agents.json` — Parallix harness configuration, outside this mission's remit.\r\n- `.gitignore` — already carries an unrelated uncommitted modification on this branch; do not add to it or revert it.\r\n- `backlog/tasks/parallix-adhoc-0001 - fix-hello-world-greeting.md` — content may be updated, but the file must not be deleted, renamed, or moved, and the `assignee` field must not be edited (the workflow records ownership itself).\r\n- `graphify-out/` — leave empty; do not generate or commit graph artifacts.\r\n- Any file outside `hello.sh`, `missions/parallix-adhoc-0001/`, and the backlog task file.\r\n\r\n## Stop Rules\r\n- Stop and report if `bash hello.sh` cannot produce exactly `Hello, World!\\n` without abandoning the `printf` form required by criterion 4.\r\n- Stop if the fix would require touching any file listed under Restricted Areas.\r\n- Stop if `git diff --stat main...HEAD -- hello.sh` exceeds 1 insertion and 1 deletion — that means the script was rewritten rather than corrected, which is out of scope.\r\n- Stop if `./scripts/verify-local.sh all` exits non-zero; do not edit the gate script to make it pass.\r\n- Stop if the intended greeting turns out to be ambiguous in a way the Risks and Assumptions section does not already resolve (for example a project convention elsewhere demanding lowercase `world`), and ask before guessing.\r\n- Stop if any work outside CP 1 and CP 2 appears necessary — this mission is a single-line string fix, and growth beyond that is a signal the scope was misread.\r\n\r\n"]
